From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] interconnect: sm6375: do not program QoS, let the boot values stand

Loading the provider with the QoS programming enabled wedges a CPU outright.
Not a fault, not an oops: CPU 3 stops answering, including to an NMI, and the
rest of the machine unravels behind it.

	rcu: INFO: rcu_preempt detected stalls on CPUs/tasks:
	rcu:  3-...0: (0 ticks this GP)
	Sending NMI from CPU 5 to CPUs 3:
	After 10 seconds, these CPUS still haven't responded to the NMI: 3
	watchdog: BUG: soft lockup - CPU#5 stuck for 11s! [rcu_preempt:15]

A core that will not take an NMI is a core parked on a bus transaction that
never completes, which is what writing a NoC QoS register belonging to a port
that is not clocked looks like from the CPU side.

The QoS port numbers here are the weakest part of this driver and were flagged
as such when it was written. The vendor holi.c expresses QoS as absolute
register offsets inside the qosbox; mainline wants a port index, and
PROGRESS.md in docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/ has the conversion written down
under a heading that starts with "CRITICAL". One wrong port and the write lands
on a neighbour's register.

None of it is needed for what this port wants from the interconnect. QoS is
priority and arbitration tuning; the bandwidth votes that keep a path alive go
to the RPM over SMD and do not touch these registers at all. The bootloader has
already programmed QoS by the time Linux starts, and every value here was
transcribed from the vendor tree anyway, so leaving those registers alone means
keeping the settings the device shipped with.

So drop ap_owned on every node, which is exactly what icc-rpm.c keys the QoS
programming off:

	if (!qn->qos.ap_owned)
		return 0;

and the provider comes up without writing a single QoS register. If someone
later wants QoS under Linux, the port numbers have to be verified one by one
against holi_qos_dump.txt first, on a device that can be recovered.

This supersedes the 0030 DIAG patch mentioned in HANDOFF-SESSION3, which did
the same thing and was lost.

